{"parentUuid":"fa98c562-30d5-4a1c-a60a-3c271e15379a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e11c3bef-3b37-47fe-a078-3380a15cf81d","agentId":"a0b3aefd808268adb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvV2NwpbzxLevGtkzDnoWs","type":"tool_result","content":"eff10d65 Merge origin/master into orca-cli-skills\nd892f61a orca: hold a startup signal until the payload can take it; tests need no setsid\n197b031c orca: bwrap ignores HUP/INT/TERM; re-raise only those deaths\nacab1d5f orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\n7db85d00 Merge origin/master into orca-cli-skills\n104b3c4f orca: forward TERM/HUP through bwrap, so `orca-ide serve` stops cleanly\n37db3ccb orca: the GUI outlives the `orca-ide open` that starts it\n4e0f5355 orca: keep --ozone-platform out of node mode; tests run under CI\n777a68b5 orca: `orca-ide` is the CLI, the GUI is `orca-ide-app`\n9e53e6cc orca: install the CLI as `orca` and six of its agent skills\n orca.nix                 | 209 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----\n tests/orca-cli-skills.sh | 261 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n tests/sandbox-fonts.sh   |   4 +-\n 3 files changed, 445 insertions(+), 29 deletions(-)\ncommit d892f61ad240f5f5fba5a2cd9bc2d05905e90ceb\nAuthor: Sauyon Lee <git@sjle.co>\nDate:   Sat Oct 24 12:28:13 2026 +0000\n\n    orca: hold a startup signal until the payload can take it; tests need no setsid\n    \n    Review round 7. With HUP/INT/TERM ignored in bwrap, every layer before\n    Node or Chromium inherits them ignored, so a TERM in the first ~200ms\n    was discarded and the launcher ran on. fwd now waits (up to 5s) while\n    the payload still has the signal ignored, then sends it.\n    \n    The tests used util-linux setsid, which CI's tool shell lacks; payloads\n    now start under bash's own job control (set -m), which gives the same\n    own-group, no-ignored-SIGINT start. New case: a TERM 50ms into startup\n    still stops the launcher.\n    \n    Comments: a group-wide HUP/TERM reaches the packaged GUI twice, and\n    Electron's one-shot handler makes the second abrupt (as the SIGKILL\n    before was) -- said plainly instead of claimed harmless; bwrap itself\n    now needs QUIT or KILL; the wait loop's three cases over 128.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: 572cb047f7ec\n\n orca.nix                 | 29 +++++++++++++++++++++++------\n tests/orca-cli-skills.sh | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------\n 2 files changed, 49 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)\ncommit d892f61ad240f5f5fba5a2cd9bc2d05905e90ceb\nAuthor: Sauyon Lee <git@sjle.co>\nDate:   Sat Oct 24 12:28:13 2026 +0000\n\n    orca: hold a startup signal until the payload can take it; tests need no setsid\n    \n    Review round 7. With HUP/INT/TERM ignored in bwrap, every layer before\n    Node or Chromium inherits them ignored, so a TERM in the first ~200ms\n    was discarded and the launcher ran on. fwd now waits (up to 5s) while\n    the payload still has the signal ignored, then sends it.\n    \n    The tests used util-linux setsid, which CI's tool shell lacks; payloads\n    now start under bash's own job control (set -m), which gives the same\n    own-group, no-ignored-SIGINT start. New case: a TERM 50ms into startup\n    still stops the launcher.\n    \n    Comments: a group-wide HUP/TERM reaches the packaged GUI twice, and\n    Electron's one-shot handler makes the second abrupt (as the SIGKILL\n    before was) -- said plainly instead of claimed harmless; bwrap itself\n    now needs QUIT or KILL; the wait loop's three cases over 128.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Entire-Checkpoint: 572cb047f7ec\n\ndiff --git a/orca.nix b/orca.nix\nindex 2f78c492..c9004670 100644\n--- a/orca.nix\n+++ b/orca.nix\n@@ -17,12 +17,17 @@ let\n   # bwrap that died of one would SIGKILL the payload (--die-with-parent)\n   # before its handler ran. (A plain exec had the same problem.) So bwrap\n   # starts with HUP, INT and TERM ignored: it only relays, and exits when\n-  # its child does. The payload is unaffected -- Node resets inherited\n-  # dispositions, and Chromium resets them and installs its own handlers.\n+  # its child does; QUIT or KILL still stops it directly. The payload is\n+  # unaffected once Node or Chromium starts -- Node resets inherited\n+  # dispositions, Chromium resets them and installs its own handlers -- and\n+  # until then fwd holds a signal rather than send it into an ignoring layer.\n   # INT is not forwarded when our group is the terminal's foreground group,\n   # since Ctrl-C has already reached the payload; the price is that a\n   # targeted `kill -INT` of a launcher in a terminal's foreground is lost.\n-  # A group-wide HUP or TERM arrives twice, which Node and Electron shrug off.\n+  # A group-wide HUP or TERM arrives twice: harmless to Node and to serve\n+  # mode, but the packaged GUI has only Electron's one-shot shutdown handler,\n+  # so the second copy ends it by default action mid-quit -- abrupt, as the\n+  # SIGKILL it replaced was, not graceful.\n   #\n   # A background job in a non-interactive shell gets stdin from /dev/null\n   # and SIGINT/SIGQUIT ignored; `<&0` undoes the first, and env sets the\n@@ -38,11 +43,23 @@ let\n         st=$(< /proc/$$/stat); read -ra st <<< \"''${st##*) }\"\n         [ \"''${st[2]}\" = \"''${st[5]}\" ] && return\n       fi\n-      kids=$(${pkgs.coreutils}/bin/cat /proc/$child/task/*/children 2>/dev/null)\n-      kill -s \"$1\" ''${kids:-$child} 2>/dev/null\n+      # Until Node or Chromium resets them, the layers inside inherit bwrap's\n+      # ignored dispositions, and a signal sent then is discarded: wait (up\n+      # to 5s) for the payload to be able to take it.\n+      bit=$(( 1 << ($(kill -l \"$1\") - 1) )) i=0 kid= ign=\n+      while :; do\n+        # No trailing newline there: read fails yet still sets kid.\n+        kid=; read -r kid _ < /proc/$child/task/$child/children 2>/dev/null\n+        [ -n \"$kid\" ] || break\n+        while read -r k v; do [ \"$k\" = SigIgn: ] && ign=$v; done < /proc/$kid/status 2>/dev/null\n+        [ -n \"$ign\" ] && (( 0x$ign & bit )) && (( i++ < 100 )) || break\n+        ${pkgs.coreutils}/bin/sleep 0.05\n+      done\n+      kill -s \"$1\" ''${kid:-$child} 2>/dev/null\n     }\n     for sig in TERM HUP INT; do trap \"fwd $sig\" $sig; done\n-    # Over 128 is a trap interrupting wait, or the child's own signal death.\n+    # Over 128 is a trap interrupting wait, the child's own signal death, or\n+    # a plain exit over 128.\n     while :; do\n       wait $child; rc=$?\n       [ $rc -gt 128 ] && kill -0 $child 2>/dev/null || break\ndiff --git a/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh b/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\nindex ca614df6..8894ff2a 100755\n--- a/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\n+++ b/tests/orca-cli-skills.sh\n@@ -86,17 +86,21 @@ else fail \"orca-ide-app node mode\" \"${out:-$(tail -1 \"$T/node-err\")}\"; fi\n # launcher a TERM kills bwrap and the process inside keeps the profile lock.\n # Node mode stands in for the GUI here: same launcher, and it can report what\n # it saw. Each payload exits on its own after 20s, so a regression cannot leak\n-# it. Started via env --default-signal, as Node's spawn would: a background\n-# job of this non-interactive shell would otherwise begin with SIGINT ignored,\n-# which bash then cannot trap. And under setsid: its own session, so no\n-# terminal however the suite is run, and its own group ($w), so a signal can\n-# go to the whole group. (setsid execs in place: $w is the launcher.)\n-payload() { # payload <out-file>: node-mode GUI launch reporting TERM/INT/HUP\n-  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 env --default-signal=INT setsid \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e '\n+# it. Started under job control (set -m; bash's own, since CI has no\n+# util-linux setsid): its own process group ($w), so a signal can go to the\n+# whole group, never a terminal's foreground group however the suite is run,\n+# and no SIGINT ignored, as bash otherwise does to a background job.\n+payload_start() { # payload_start <out-file>: node-mode GUI launch reporting TERM/INT/HUP\n+  set -m\n+  ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e '\n     for (const s of [\"SIGTERM\", \"SIGINT\", \"SIGHUP\"])\n       process.on(s, () => { console.log(\"got-\" + s); process.exit(0); });\n     console.log(\"ready\"); setTimeout(() => process.exit(3), 20000);' >\"$1\" 2>&1 &\n   w=$!\n+  set +m\n+}\n+payload() { # payload <out-file>: payload_start, then wait for it to be ready\n+  payload_start \"$1\"\n   for _ in $(seq 150); do grep -q ready \"$1\" && break; sleep 0.2; done\n }\n \n@@ -123,7 +127,8 @@ if grep -q got-SIGINT \"$T/int\" && [ \"$rc\" -eq 0 ]; then\n   pass \"INT to the GUI launcher (no terminal) reaches the process in the sandbox\"\n else fail \"INT forwarding\" \"rc=$rc, saw: $(tr '\\n' ' ' < \"$T/int\")\"; fi\n \n-# TERM to the whole group (a terminal's HUP, a control-group stop) reaches\n+# TERM to the whole group (a control-group stop; a terminal hangup is the\n+# same with HUP) reaches\n # bwrap too; it must survive to let the payload's handler run.\n payload \"$T/group\"\n kill -TERM -- -\"$w\" 2>/dev/null; wait \"$w\"; rc=$?\n@@ -131,6 +136,18 @@ if grep -q got-SIGTERM \"$T/group\" && [ \"$rc\" -eq 0 ]; then\n   pass \"TERM to the launcher's whole group lets the payload handle it\"\n else fail \"group TERM\" \"rc=$rc, saw: $(tr '\\n' ' ' < \"$T/group\")\"; fi\n \n+# A TERM during startup, while the layers before Node still have it ignored\n+# (bwrap's dispositions are inherited until Node resets them), must not be\n+# dropped: the launcher has to end, by the handler or by the default action.\n+payload_start \"$T/early\"\n+sleep 0.05; kill -TERM \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\n+for _ in $(seq 50); do kill -0 \"$w\" 2>/dev/null || break; sleep 0.2; done\n+if kill -0 \"$w\" 2>/dev/null; then\n+  fail \"early TERM\" \"launcher still running 10s after a TERM at startup\"\n+  kill -KILL \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\n+else pass \"a TERM during startup still stops the launcher\"; fi\n+wait \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\n+\n # Only HUP/INT/TERM deaths are re-raised: a plain exit above 128 is a status,\n # and re-raising 148 (TSTP) would stop the launcher instead of exiting it.\n ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 timeout 60 \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" -e 'process.exit(148)' >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?\n@@ -190,7 +207,7 @@ if grep -q '/bin/bwrap' \"$gui_launcher\" 2>/dev/null && grep -q -- '--die-with-pa\n   pass \"the GUI's bwrap dies with its launcher\"\n else fail \"GUI launcher\" \"$gui_launcher lacks --die-with-parent (or is missing)\"; fi\n # And the payload outlives a parent that spawns it detached and exits.\n-bash -c 'ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 setsid \"$1\" -e \"\n+bash -c 'ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 \"$1\" -e \"\n   setTimeout(() => require(\\\"fs\\\").writeFileSync(process.argv[1], \\\"alive\\\"), 3000);\n   setTimeout(() => process.exit(0), 4000);\" \"$2\" >/dev/null 2>&1 &' _ \"$GUI/bin/orca-ide-app\" \"$T/alive\"\n for _ in $(seq 75); do [ -s \"$T/alive\" ] && break; sleep 0.2; done","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d2dc0fe7-1603-4565-b48a-bd5b24c24986","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:29:24.826Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"eff10d65 Merge origin/master into orca-cli-skills\nd892f61a orca: hold a startup signal until the payload can take it; tests need no setsid\n197b031c orca: bwrap ignores HUP/INT/TERM; re-raise only those deaths\nacab1d5f orca: forward INT off a terminal, survive Ctrl-C on one; GUI dies with its launcher\n7db85d00 Merge origin/master into orca-cli-skills\n104b3c4f orca: forward TERM/HUP through bwrap, so `orca-ide serve` stops cleanly\n37db3ccb orca: the GUI outlives the `orca-ide open` that starts it\n4e0f5355 orca: keep --ozone-platform out of node mode; tests run under CI\n777a68b5 orca: `orca-ide` is the CLI, the GUI is `orca-ide-app`\n9e53e6cc orca: install the CLI as `orca` and six of its agent skills\n orca.nix                 | 209 ++++++++++++