{"parentUuid":"8d4fb0e9-6126-4eda-aada-3c93e33df52e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0b3aefd808268adb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgM4wUuZQ1ryyZyqJX1MJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":60957,"output_tokens":2535,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":343},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1250},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2535,"cache_read_input_tokens":60957,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1250,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":3812,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"2e049f36-addf-430c-a582-deb6373df3b5","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgM4wUTWruZeyeZeEMRaw","requestRef":"68b640d2-6d28-4e90-8632-c17c1f73b1a2","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"Explore","type":"assistant","uuid":"60942cf2-0f30-4d34-b18b-455f05ad9470","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:33:31.810Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/sauyon/devel/dotfiles/.drovr/wt/orca-cli-skills","sessionId":"d19ff9ff-38c8-4378-ac54-4f4061f74300","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"orca-cli-skills"}
{"parentUuid":"60942cf2-0f30-4d34-b18b-455f05ad9470","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0b3aefd808268adb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgM4wUuZQ1ryyZyqJX1MJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RKpZuSK4HF3DDLF5rkxBKU","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Round 8 review of d892f61a: one Important finding, no Critical, three nits.\n\nBuilt outputs checked: GUI `/nix/store/q0a61lk4...-orca-1.4.238` (the nixGL wrapper execs `j0qfjf54.../bin/orca-ide-app`, which is the forwardSignals launcher) and CLI `gw1hq78w...-orca-cli-1.4.238`. bwrap runs without `--unshare-pid`, and the chain bwrap child → container-init → /init (bash) → appimage-exec.sh → AppRun → orca-ide execs all the way down. I watched that with node-mode runs I started and stopped myself.\n\n**Critical**\nNone.\n\n**Important**\n\n1. orca.nix:53. A TERM/HUP/INT that arrives before bwrap has a child is silently dropped, and the launcher keeps running.\n   - `[ -n \"$kid\" ] || break` treats \"no child of $child\" as \"ready\" and sends the signal to `$child`.\n   - But until bwrap's clone, `$child` is env, then the `.orca-ide-unwrapped` bash preamble (its rootfs/etc loops), then bwrap itself. All three now have HUP/INT/TERM ignored (`env --ignore-signal`), so the signal is discarded.\n   - The startup log only accepts \"a signal arriving before bwrap has forked its child goes to bwrap\". When that was written, bwrap died of it and the launcher ended. Now the signal is lost and the launcher runs on, which is worse than documented. The window is also longer: from the trap being set (about 15-20ms here) to bwrap's clone (about 35-45ms).\n   - Reproduced with a payload that exits 3 after 2-4s. TERM sent to the launcher at 10ms, 15ms, 20ms, 30ms and 35ms gave rc=3 (dropped; the payload ran to its own timeout) in 3 of 10 and 2 of 9 starts. At the 30ms drop, a sample just before the kill showed `child=.orca-ide-unwrapped kids=[]`. From 40ms on it was always rc=0 (got-SIGTERM).\n   - Cheap fix: check the SigIgn of `t=${kid:-$child}` instead of breaking when kid is empty. Read `/proc/$t/status` and keep looping while the bit is set; send to `$t`. The preamble's `$(readlink)` subshells inherit the ignore, so that phase also waits correctly. The only cost is up to 5s in fwd if bwrap outlives its child, and bwrap exits when its child does.\n   - The new test (tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-149) sends at 50ms, after this window, so it cannot catch this.\n\n**Nits**\n\n2. orca.nix:52 and :54. The `2>/dev/null` after `< /proc/...` does not hide the open error. Redirections are applied left to right, so a missing file prints `line N: /proc/.../children: No such file or directory` on the launcher's stderr. Confirmed in bash 5.3. It happens when bwrap or the kid exits during fwd, or on a kernel without CONFIG_PROC_CHILDREN (where forwarding then silently goes only to bwrap, as before). Fix: `2>/dev/null < file`, or wrap it in `{ ...; } 2>/dev/null`.\n\n3. orca.nix:49. `bit i kid ign` are globals, and bash does run traps nested: a TERM, a HUP and a second TERM sent while a handler was sleeping each entered fwd before any of them returned (tested in bash 5.3). A signal arriving during a 5s hold runs its own loop inside the outer one and shares `i`/`bit`.\n   - Effects: the signals are delivered last-in-first-out. If the inner call timed out (i=101), the outer one sends at once, but the payload is still ignoring then, so that signal is lost either way.\n   - Within this window it is mostly harmless, because Node resets all three at the same moment. `local bit i kid ign k v` is free.\n   - `ign` is also never reset per iteration. If a status read fails, the stale value from the last read (still ignoring) makes it sleep once more instead of breaking. That is harmless, since the next read of children comes back empty.\n\n4. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-149, the early-TERM case can pass without testing anything.\n   - If the TERM lands before the launcher's trap is set (about 15-20ms here; a slow CI runner could push that past 50ms), the launcher dies by default action and bwrap dies with its parent, so the case passes even without the fix.\n   - It accepts any death, so it cannot tell the hold working from that race. On this machine 50ms does land in the hold window (kid = container-init / appimage-exec.sh with SigIgn 0x4003), so it does test the hold here.\n   - Suggestion: also try a short delay such as 25-30ms, which would expose finding 1, or report whether got-SIGTERM was seen.\n\n**Checks that came out clean**\n- `kill -l HUP/INT/TERM` gives 1/2/15, so the bits are 0x1/0x2/0x4000, correct.\n- The pid is stable across the exec layers. container-init's ldconfig is a grandchild and never read, and the `$kid` read always comes from bwrap's direct child. Node's reset showed up as SigIgn going 0x4003 → 0x1001000 at about 170ms, ending the loop.\n- A kid that exits mid-loop leaves children empty, so the loop breaks.\n- The main `wait` loop resumes correctly after a long fwd.\n- The INT foreground check is unchanged and still runs before the hold.\n- `set -m` gives the payload its own pgrp, not foreground, both with no tty and on a pty. That held whether the script was in the foreground or a background job, with no SIGTTOU stops and no job notices printed.\n- CI tool shell: every external command the test uses (cat, uname, mktemp, readlink, grep, awk, sed, timeout, tail, head, seq, sleep, tr, ls, paste, jq, sha256sum, wc, env, plus nix/nix-store from the image) is covered by bash, coreutils, gnugrep, gnused, gawk and jq. `setsid` is gone, and python3 is optional (the case is skipped without it).\n- tests/sandbox-fonts.sh changes: fine."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":60957,"output_tokens":2535,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":343},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1250},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2535,"cache_read_input_tokens":60957,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1250,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01RKpZuSK4HF3DDLF5rkxBKU":{"message":"Round 8 review of d892f61a: one Important finding, no Critical, three nits.\n\nBuilt outputs checked: GUI `/nix/store/q0a61lk4...-orca-1.4.238` (the nixGL wrapper execs `j0qfjf54.../bin/orca-ide-app`, which is the forwardSignals launcher) and CLI `gw1hq78w...-orca-cli-1.4.238`. bwrap runs without `--unshare-pid`, and the chain bwrap child → container-init → /init (bash) → appimage-exec.sh → AppRun → orca-ide execs all the way down. I watched that with node-mode runs I started and stopped myself.\n\n**Critical**\nNone.\n\n**Important**\n\n1. orca.nix:53. A TERM/HUP/INT that arrives before bwrap has a child is silently dropped, and the launcher keeps running.\n   - `[ -n \"$kid\" ] || break` treats \"no child of $child\" as \"ready\" and sends the signal to `$child`.\n   - But until bwrap's clone, `$child` is env, then the `.orca-ide-unwrapped` bash preamble (its rootfs/etc loops), then bwrap itself. All three now have HUP/INT/TERM ignored (`env --ignore-signal`), so the signal is discarded.\n   - The startup log only accepts \"a signal arriving before bwrap has forked its child goes to bwrap\". When that was written, bwrap died of it and the launcher ended. Now the signal is lost and the launcher runs on, which is worse than documented. The window is also longer: from the trap being set (about 15-20ms here) to bwrap's clone (about 35-45ms).\n   - Reproduced with a payload that exits 3 after 2-4s. TERM sent to the launcher at 10ms, 15ms, 20ms, 30ms and 35ms gave rc=3 (dropped; the payload ran to its own timeout) in 3 of 10 and 2 of 9 starts. At the 30ms drop, a sample just before the kill showed `child=.orca-ide-unwrapped kids=[]`. From 40ms on it was always rc=0 (got-SIGTERM).\n   - Cheap fix: check the SigIgn of `t=${kid:-$child}` instead of breaking when kid is empty. Read `/proc/$t/status` and keep looping while the bit is set; send to `$t`. The preamble's `$(readlink)` subshells inherit the ignore, so that phase also waits correctly. The only cost is up to 5s in fwd if bwrap outlives its child, and bwrap exits when its child does.\n   - The new test (tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-149) sends at 50ms, after this window, so it cannot catch this.\n\n**Nits**\n\n2. orca.nix:52 and :54. The `2>/dev/null` after `< /proc/...` does not hide the open error. Redirections are applied left to right, so a missing file prints `line N: /proc/.../children: No such file or directory` on the launcher's stderr. Confirmed in bash 5.3. It happens when bwrap or the kid exits during fwd, or on a kernel without CONFIG_PROC_CHILDREN (where forwarding then silently goes only to bwrap, as before). Fix: `2>/dev/null < file`, or wrap it in `{ ...; } 2>/dev/null`.\n\n3. orca.nix:49. `bit i kid ign` are globals, and bash does run traps nested: a TERM, a HUP and a second TERM sent while a handler was sleeping each entered fwd before any of them returned (tested in bash 5.3). A signal arriving during a 5s hold runs its own loop inside the outer one and shares `i`/`bit`.\n   - Effects: the signals are delivered last-in-first-out. If the inner call timed out (i=101), the outer one sends at once, but the payload is still ignoring then, so that signal is lost either way.\n   - Within this window it is mostly harmless, because Node resets all three at the same moment. `local bit i kid ign k v` is free.\n   - `ign` is also never reset per iteration. If a status read fails, the stale value from the last read (still ignoring) makes it sleep once more instead of breaking. That is harmless, since the next read of children comes back empty.\n\n4. tests/orca-cli-skills.sh:142-149, the early-TERM case can pass without testing anything.\n   - If the TERM lands before the launcher's trap is set (about 15-20ms here; a slow CI runner could push that past 50ms), the launcher dies by default action and bwrap dies with its parent, so the case passes even without the fix.\n   - It accepts any death, so it cannot tell the hold working from that race. On this machine 50ms does land in the hold window (kid = container-init / appimage-exec.sh with SigIgn 0x4003), so it does test the hold here.\n   - Suggestion: also try a short delay such as 25-30ms, which would expose finding 1, or report whether got-SIGTERM was seen.\n\n**Checks that came out clean**\n- `kill -l HUP/INT/TERM` gives 1/2/15, so the bits are 0x1/0x2/0x4000, correct.\n- The pid is stable across the exec layers. container-init's ldconfig is a grandchild and never read, and the `$kid` read always comes from bwrap's direct child. Node's reset showed up as SigIgn going 0x4003 → 0x1001000 at about 170ms, ending the loop.\n- A kid that exits mid-loop leaves children empty, so the loop breaks.\n- The main `wait` loop resumes correctly after a long fwd.\n- The INT foreground check is unchanged and still runs before the hold.\n- `set -m` gives the payload its own pgrp, not foreground, both with no tty and on a pty. That held whether the script was in the foreground or a background job, with no SIGTTOU stops and no job notices printed.\n- CI tool shell: every external command the test uses (cat, uname, mktemp, readlink, grep, awk, sed, timeout, tail, head, seq, sleep, tr, ls, paste, jq, sha256sum, wc, env, plus nix/nix-store from the image) is covered by bash, coreutils, gnugrep, gnused, gawk and jq. `setsid` is gone, and python3 is optional (the case is skipped without it).\n- tests/sandbox-fonts.sh changes: fine."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"2e049f36-addf-430c-a582-deb6373df3b5","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgM4wUTWruZeyeZeEMRaw","requestRef":"68b640d2-6d28-4e90-8632-c17c1f73b1a2","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"Explore","type":"assistant","uuid":"e70b04de-29d6-442d-8292-a68dd071711d","timestamp":"2026-10-24T12:33:53.393Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/sauyon/devel/dotfiles/.drovr/wt/orca-cli-skills","sessionId":"d19ff9ff-38c8-4378-ac54-4f4061f74300","version":"2.1.308","gitBranch":"orca-cli-skills"}
